#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142636 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe5587f6-f6a7-523c-b532-15786ade73b7" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Whole Number by a Non-Unit Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "23532ee6-f05b-5ac7-b722-84891afd3bc5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14197" :question/problem "![](images/45/45d4e4ca4f1cdfddcb624dccf32cb053818a2fd70cbb7b5a02d1ff30f9784e2b.png)\n\nThe model above represents $3$ wholes. Use the model to determine the missing number in the following division problem.\n$0$" :question/worked-solution "We proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we split each whole into fifths.\n\n![](images/9c/9c10c283125b23a553855e247149fba126c1977bf62a487b09121eb539dabfbf.png)\n\n- Next, we create groups of ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac3{5}}}$.\n\n![](images/a8/a8ee616adc072552e64182bba65bd74754b4a16857bfc7fe7e411ab9784bd331.png)\n\n- Then, we count the groups.\n\n![](images/ad/ad9c3bcb60e6277ae536b00489b3f4267a31fb9f2d0a7af368ee0cc43215287d.png)\n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}3$ wholes into groups of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\dfrac3{5}}}\n$$\n\nwe get ${\\color{purple}{5}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n3 \\div \\dfrac{3}{{5}} = \\fbox{5}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{purple}5}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142637 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c692a59-11d5-5b7c-868c-438cfd23a04f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Missing Numbers"}] :question/id #uuid "be59814b-3823-57b9-9a2c-f678e1d34183" :question/math-academy-id "e-14203" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c3f53204a0271ea9816eaddbdbeeb84648638918531ace2f06251af765fe05f.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing numbers in the following division problem.\n$0^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $\\color{blue}2$ wholes. Each whole is split into *thirds*.\n\n![](images/d9/d9c692ed44d98c47b475dc18c2d28a01d0ab1f2f231ed24a9604f14000f599b1.png)\n\n- The thirds are grouped, and each group contains ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac23}}$.\n\n![](images/3c/3c9fd6222f68deffa02ce69a2f31dca220ce2285f71dbd8255d364126ea0d8c8.png)\n\n- There are $\\color{purple} 3$ groups in total.\n\n![](images/22/228c9e5eea2b5696d64d4423ecd927e12a11e6672d614cc30a947549943be0c4.png)\n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}2$ wholes into groups of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\dfrac23}}\n$$\n\nwe get ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n$2$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ and $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{purple}3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142655 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f7df9be-867f-56b1-85d3-00ad838dc054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Number"}] :question/id #uuid "7f3801c4-d25f-57e0-85b3-b52df08d7072" :question/math-academy-id "e-15654" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7fbc79b1d259f645a8296be12c2748e5abb05d02217f3628e24ef20781f06c71.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the following division problem.\n$\\frac{0}{0}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $\\color{blue}4$ wholes. Each whole is split into *fifths*.\n\n![](images/20/2096f3451d19fc44bccca9ebe3033e9af29878aa928ad05b102e391be3f76105.png)\n\n- The fifths are grouped, and each group contains ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac{2}{5}}}$.\n\n![](images/96/96bc693ffe7fa65c90758e0d86a7176b19f7a279cfff12607926e0350448fe42.png)\n\n- There are $\\color{purple} 10$ groups in total.\n\n![](images/1e/1ebdc6bce6be33bbdd5fd7ff79a562c22e68103cebc8f0ded5ddac6833633989.png)\n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}4$ wholes into groups of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\dfrac{2}{5}}}\n$$\n\nwe get ${\\color{purple}{10}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\dfrac{2}{5}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c692a59-11d5-5b7c-868c-438cfd23a04f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Missing Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "f318e366-a203-572f-8e10-c1869f8b50df" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140500} {:answer-field/id #uuid "1565e45a-71c0-504e-bdd4-c8c334958932" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140501}] :question/id #uuid "1a3a822b-3969-57b3-aa0a-af3e5b1eaad6" :question/math-academy-id "q-237516" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8f9519928fa02dc177e03d7edc01ca07d57d5b9052d3575ce90a5523003c2e7.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing numbers in the following division problem.$4 \\div$\n{{field-1}}\n$=$\n{{field-2}}"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142847 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe5587f6-f6a7-523c-b532-15786ade73b7" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Whole Number by a Non-Unit Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "2ec19b71-3b3a-5435-8cc7-7348e2f13fc3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186140510}] :question/id #uuid "81a263a9-d502-559d-acde-febf3ffe6377" :question/math-academy-id "q-237553" :question/problem "![](images/45/45d4e4ca4f1cdfddcb624dccf32cb053818a2fd70cbb7b5a02d1ff30f9784e2b.png)\n\nIf the model above represents $3$ wholes, use the model to solve the following division problem. Express your answer as a whole number.$3 \\div \\frac{3}{7} =$\n{{field-1}}"}]}
